      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "### Decrementing `nextId` when an object is destroyed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nEarlier, developers used the `finalize()` method to perform cleanup when an object was garbage collected.\n\nIf we wanted to decrement `nextId` when an object was destroyed, we could override the `finalize()` method because it is inherited from the `Object` class.\n\n```java\n@Override\nprotected void finalize() {\n    nextId--;\n}\n```\n\nHowever, this approach is **not reliable** because:\n\n* The **Garbage Collector (GC)** decides when to reclaim memory.\n* Calling `System.gc()` **only requests** garbage collection; it does **not guarantee** that GC will run.\n* Even if GC runs, it does **not guarantee** that `finalize()` will be executed immediately, or at all.\n\nEarlier, programmers sometimes wrote:\n\n```java\nSystem.gc();              // Request the JVM to perform garbage collection\nSystem.runFinalization(); // Request execution of pending finalizers\n```\n\nBut these methods should **not** be relied upon.\n\nMoreover:\n\n* `finalize()` has been **deprecated since Java 9**.\n* It has been **removed from modern Java versions (including JDK 25)**.\n* Therefore, `finalize()` should **never** be used in new code.\n\n### Best Practice\n\nIf you need to decrease `nextId`, create an explicit method such as:\n\n```java\npublic void deleteEmployee() {\n    nextId--;\n}\n```\n\nand call it whenever an employee is intentionally removed.\n\n> **Note:** In real-world applications, IDs are usually never reused. Therefore, `nextId` is normally **not decremented** even when an object is deleted.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "### Decrementing `nextId` when an object is destroyed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nEarlier, developers used the `finalize()` method to perform cleanup when an object was garbage collected.\n\nIf we wanted to decrement `nextId` when an object was destroyed, we could override the `finalize()` method because it is inherited from the `Object` class.\n\n```java\n@Override\nprotected void finalize() {\n    nextId--;\n}\n```\n\nHowever, this approach is **not reliable** because:\n\n* The **Garbage Collector (GC)** decides when to reclaim memory.\n* Calling `System.gc()` **only requests** garbage collection; it does **not guarantee** that GC will run.\n* Even if GC runs, it does **not guarantee** that `finalize()` will be executed immediately, or at all.\n\nEarlier, programmers sometimes wrote:\n\n```java\nSystem.gc();              // Request the JVM to perform garbage collection\nSystem.runFinalization(); // Request execution of pending finalizers\n```\n\nBut these methods should **not** be relied upon.\n\nMoreover:\n\n* `finalize()` has been **deprecated since Java 9**.\n* It has been **removed from modern Java versions (including JDK 25)**.\n* Therefore, `finalize()` should **never** be used in new code.\n\n### Best Practice\n\nIf you need to decrease `nextId`, create an explicit method such as:\n\n```java\npublic void deleteEmployee() {\n    nextId--;\n}\n```\n\nand call it whenever an employee is intentionally removed.\n\n> **Note:** In real-world applications, IDs are usually never reused. Therefore, `nextId` is normally **not decremented** even when an object is deleted.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
